{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>Let the waters swarm with swarming living creatures, and let fowl fly.</b> And further on it is said that the fowl were created from the earth, and our Sages of blessed memory said (Ḥullin 27b) by way of interpretation that they were created from the mud. It follows that the fowl are intermediate between the beasts, which were created from the earth, and the fish, which were created from the waters. This indicates that these three species were created only for the sake of man, to be his servants. A hint to the matter: ״עֶבֶד״ (servant) is an acrostic of עוֹף, בְּהֵמָה, דָּגִים (fowl, beast, fish) — for HaShem gave him everything as servants, and man has dominion in the sea and on dry land and in both together, so that man should find behind him nothing at all that was not given into his authority." "And by way of allusion, we find classes of men who trace back to these three species, for we find that the wicked who incline after the earthly portion were most likened to ״כַּבְּהֵמוֹת נִדְמוּ״ (they are likened to beasts), for they too — their origin is from dust. And the righteous were likened to water, whose matter is finer than the matter of earth, and they were likened to fish whose essential vitality is in the water. And the intermediate ones were likened to the fowl that fly between heaven and earth — that is, those stationed between spirituality and corporeality — and whose matter is from water and earth, as stated. And it appears that for this reason a doubled blessing, ״פְּרוּ וּרְבוּ״ (be fruitful and multiply), was said of the fish, just as He mentioned concerning the species of man. And concerning the fowl only one blessing was said, ״וְהָעוֹף יִרֶב בָּאָרֶץ״ (and let the fowl multiply on the earth); but concerning the beasts no blessing was said at all. And all of this is self-evident to one who reflects, and it accords with the words of our Sages of blessed memory (Midrash Aggadah Genesis 1) that no blessing was said concerning beasts and wild animals because of the serpent, etc. — for indeed the evil impulse, whose power derives from the primordial serpent, lies in wait there among the class of the wicked who were likened to beasts and wild animals of the earth; and would that they diminish and not multiply. And this will be explained further in the portion of Beha'alotekha (Numbers 11:1), that the rabble that was among them — that is, the intermediate ones — asked for meat and fish, which are not of their species, and they were given the flesh of fowl — each species found its counterpart."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T23:33:31.670219172Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}